<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tungabhadra River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tungabhadra is a river of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, formed by the junction of the Tunga and Bhadra rivers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, that flows north-east for about 530 km to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>. Vijayanagara (Hampi), the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>, was built on its southern bank in the fourteenth century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raichur-doab">Raichur Doab</a> between the Tungabhadra and the Krishna was long contested by Vijayanagara and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-sultanates">Deccan sultanates</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tungabhadra-river.jpg" alt="Tungabhadra River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vyacheslav Argenberg · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hampi,_Tungabhadra_River,_India.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name joins two streams, the Tunga and the Bhadra, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> tunga, high, and bhadra, auspicious. Both rise at Gangamoola in the Varaha Parvatha of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a>, at an elevation of 1,458 metres, and flow down the eastern slope. The Tunga runs about 147 km and the Bhadra a little more before they meet at Koodli near Shimoga. The joined river then meanders through the plains for about 531 km.</p><p>Of that length, 382 km lie in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, 58 km form the boundary between Karnataka and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>, and 91 km run in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>. The catchment covers 69,552 square kilometres. The river is perennial and is influenced chiefly by the south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a>, though its summer flow dwindles sharply. Near Hampi it has cut a narrow gorge through piles of granite. The wedge of land between the Tungabhadra and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> is known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raichur-doab">Raichur Doab</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> tradition holds that the two source rivers were formed from the sweat of Varaha, the third incarnation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, who rested on the peak after killing the demon Hiranyaksha. Many holy places stand along the banks: the Sringeri Sharada Peetham founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adi-shankara">Adi Shankara</a> on the Tunga, the Virupaksha Temple at Hampi, Nava Brindavana, an island with the resting places of nine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhvacharya">Madhva</a> saints, and the Sangameswaram Temple where the river meets the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>. The ruins of Vijayanagara and Hampi, the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>, overlook the river, and Hampi is listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnadevaraya">Krishnadevaraya</a> built stone flood-protection walls along the banks between 1525 and 1527, from Sringeri to near Kurnool, using very large boulders; they are still intact. The British engineer Arthur Cotton built the Sunkesula Barrage near Kurnool around 1860, first intended for navigation and later used for irrigation through the K. C. Canal. In the middle of the twentieth century the Tungabhadra Dam was built at Hospet to supply agriculture and industry; its architect was Malur Srinivasa Thirumale Iyengar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The river still supplies irrigation, power and drinking water, with further dams at Gajanur on the Tunga and Lakkavalli on the Bhadra. The Tungabhadra Dam holds a storage capacity of 135 Tmcft, reduced by siltation by about 30 Tmcft. Industry and mining along its banks produce effluents that have polluted its water and harmed its fisheries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tungabhadra River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tughluq armies failed to recover the region, and by his death in 1358 Bahman Shah controlled a kingdom stretching from the Arabian Sea coast to the borders of Telangana, bounded in the south by the Krishna and <b>Tungabhadra</b> rivers, where it faced the newly founded Vijayanagara Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its early decades were spent fighting the neighbouring Deccan sultanates of Ahmadnagar, Golconda and Bidar, and the Vijayanagara Empire to the south over the fertile Raichur doab between the Krishna and <b>Tungabhadra</b> rivers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Chalukya kingdom rested on the agriculture of the Deccan plateau and the valleys of the Krishna, Malaprabha and <b>Tungabhadra</b> rivers, where millets, pulses, cotton and, on irrigated land, rice were grown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The armies of Alauddin Khalji and Muhammad bin Tughluq had destroyed or humbled the old kingdoms of the Yadavas of Devagiri, the Kakatiyas of Warangal and the Pandyas of Madurai, and in 1327 annexed the small kingdom of Kampili on the <b>Tungabhadra River</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Someshvara I, stricken by incurable illness in 1068, ended his life by drowning in the <b>Tungabhadra</b> river, a death recorded by the poet Bilhana and in inscriptions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Gadwal was established around 1663 by Pedda Soma Bhupal, a local chief who built the fort at Gadwal in the land between the Krishna and <b>Tungabhadra</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/gadwal-samasthan">Gadwal Samasthan</a></li></ul></section>
